## ABSTRACT
Perovskite solar cells (PSCs) have rapidly developed in the past few years, with a record efficiency exceeding \(25\%\). However, the long-term stability of PSCs remains a challenge and limits their practical application. Many high-performance PSCs have an n-i-p device architecture employing 4-tert-butylpyridine (t-BP) and bis(trifluoromethane)sulfonimide lithium salt (Li-TFSI) as bidopants for the hole-transporting layer (HTL). However, the hygroscopicity of Li-TFSI and low boiling point of t-BP negatively impacts the moisture stability of these PSC devices. Herein, we report the use of the fluorine-containing hydrophobic compound tris(pentafluorophenyl)phosphine (35FP) as a dopant for poly[bis(4-phenyl)(2,4,6-trimethylphenyl)amine] (PTAA). With better hydrophobicity and stability than undoped PTAA, a PSC device containing 35FP-doped PTAA demonstrated improved charge transport properties and reduced trap density, leading to a significant enhancement in performance. In addition, the long-term stability of a 35FP-doped PTAA PSC under air exposure without encapsulation was demonstrated, with \(80\%\) of the initial device efficiency maintained for \(1,000\;\mathrm{h}\). This work provides a new approach for the fabrication of efficient and stable PSCs to explore hydrophobic dopants as a substitute for hydrophilic Li-TFSI/t-BP.
## KEYWORDS
perovskite solar cells, additive engineering, long-term stability, hydrophobic dopant
## 1 Introduction
In recent years, perovskite (PSK) materials have achieved a breathtaking pace of development for use in metal halide perovskite solar cells (PSCs) due to their extraordinary advantages including a high light absorption coefficient [1], adjustable band gap [2], long charge diffusion length [3], and defect tolerance [4]. As of May 6, 2021, the highest certified power conversion efficiency (PCE) of a PSC was \(25.5\%\) [5]. The rapid improvement in PSC efficiency can be attributed to the modification of PSK materials [6–8], device structure, and interface design [9–11]. These advantages demonstrate that PSCs are an extremely promising candidate for next-generation thin-film photovoltaic applications.
Although the reported PCE of PSCs has rapidly improved, the problem of long-term PSK stability needs to be solved to enable mass production and practical large-scale application [12, 13]. PSK materials are irreversibly destroyed by exposure to moisture in air [14, 15]. Thus, improving the moisture stability of PSCs that are exposed to air is a major focus of PSK research. A large amount of work has been performed that reports the doping of the PSK intrinsic layer [16–18] and the modification of the interface between the carrier transport layer and the PSK layer [19–21] to balance charge transport and improve PSK quality. However, PSC stability is not just related to charge transport and PSK quality, but also to the properties of the carrier transport layer and its additives [22]. Unfortunately, most researchers have ignored the effects of hole transport layer (HTL) dopants on the stability of PSCs, so little research has been reported in this area.
little research has been reported in this area.
Poly[bis(4-phenyl)(2,4,6-trimethylphenyl)amine] (PTAA) is a hole transport material that shows excellent performance when used in PSCs. Due to its impressive hole transport performance and energy levels that match those of PSK, PTAA is an ideal hole material [23–25]. However, as an organic polymer semiconductor, PTAA also has poor conductivity. In practical applications, it is necessary to introduce additives into PTAA to improve its performance [26]. Two commonly used additives, bis(trifluoromethane)sulfonimide lithium salt (Li-TFSI) and 4-tert-butylpyridine (t-BP), have been reported to improve the conductivity of HTLs [27]. However, Li-TFSI is deliquescent and hygroscopic, allowing the structure of HTL and perovskite films to be rapidly degraded and destroyed by absorbed water molecules [28, 29]. Furthermore, Li<sup>+</sup> can diffuse through PSK films and accumulate in the electron transport layer (ETL) due to its small atomic radius and high diffusivity [30, 31]. The boiling point of t-BP is relatively low, which also contributes to PSC instability. During device manufacturing and long-term storage, t-BP easily evaporates [32, 33]. Thus, the addition of Li-TFSI and t-BP has an adverse effect on the stability of PSC devices. Based on these negative factors, it is necessary to develop new HTL dopants to replace the commonly used Li-TFSI/t-BP bi-doped and undoped HTLs as soon as possible.
In 2015, Huang et al. doped F4-TCNQ into PTAA at different concentrations to reduce series resistance and improve the PCE of
their PSC device [34]. In 2018, Jia et al. increased an HTL's charge extraction ability by doping LAD into PTAA, improving the stability of their device and reducing \(J{-}V\) hysteresis [35]. In 2019, by comparing the device performance of PSCs containing PTAA doped with CuSCN or CuI, Lee et al. found that the efficiency of CuSCN-doped devices was more significantly enhanced [36].
In this work, we report an n-i-p type PSC device with an \(\mathrm{ITO/SnO_{2}/P S K/P T A A/A g}\) structure. The PTAA HTL doped with fluorine-containing tris(pentafluorophenyl)phosphine (35FP) achieved uniform and pinhole-free HTL films. Due to the 35FP doping strategy, the PSC devices reported herein demonstrated improved charge transport properties and reduced trap density, leading to a significantly enhanced PCE of \(20.45\%\) . Additionally, the devices maintained \(80\%\) of their initial efficiency after \(1{,}000\;\mathrm{h}\) under ambient conditions without encapsulation. The stability of these PSC devices was significantly enhanced by 35FP doping compared with Li-TFSI/1-BP doping or using undoped PTAA. Overall, the main benefits of 35FP-doped HTLs were an improvement in charge extraction, reduction of trap density, and inhibition of charge recombination. This work demonstrates a simple doping method for hole transport materials which significantly improves the efficiency and stability of PSC devices.
## 2 Experimental section
## 2.1 Materials
Dimethylformamide (DMF, \(99.9\%\) ), dimethyl sulfoxide (DMSO, \(99.9\%\) ), isopropanol (IPA, \(99.7\%\) ), chlorobenzene (CB), lead iodide \((\mathrm{PbI}_{2},\) \(99.999\%\) ), formamidine iodide (FAI, \(99.9\%\) ), methylammonium iodide (MAI, \(99.9\%\) ), and methylammonium chloride (MACl, \(99.5\%\) ) were purchased from Advanced Election Technology. Tris(pentafluorophenyl)phosphine (35FP) was purchased from Acros Organics. Tin oxide \(\left(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\right)\) colloidal dispersion ( \(15\%\) in \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})\) was purchased from Alfa Aesar. Poly[bis(4-phenyl)(2,4,6-Trimethylphenyl)amine] (PTAA) was purchased from Xi'an Polymer Light Technology.
## 2.2 Devices fabrication
For PSC devices: Patterned indium tin oxide (ITO) glass was cleaned by sonication in acetone, ethanol, and deionized water for \(15~\mathrm{min}\) . The ITO glass was then dried with a nitrogen gun and cleaned by UV-ozone for \(30\ \mathrm{min}\) . \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) films were prepared by spin-coating diluted \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) colloidal solution \((0.1~\mathrm{~mL~}~\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) colloidal dispersion diluted in \(0.6~\mathrm{mL}\) deionized water) at 3,000 rpm for \(30~\mathrm{s},\) followed by annealing at \(160~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(30\ \mathrm{min}\) in air. The \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) substrates were cleaned by UV–ozone for \(15~\mathrm{min}\) and transferred to a glovebox for the next steps. Perovskite films were obtained by a two-step spin-coating method. \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) \(\left(692~\mathrm{mg}\right)\) in 1 mL DMF and DMSO \((91,\nu/v)\) was spin-coated onto the \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) films at a rate of \(1{,}500\ \mathrm{rpm}\) for \(30~\mathrm{s}\) . A solution of FAI \((90~\mathrm{mg})\) , MAI \((9\;\mathrm{mg})\) , and MACl \((9\;\mathrm{mg})\) in \(1~\mathrm{mL}\) IPA was spin-coated on the \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) films at \(3{,}000\;\mathrm{rpm}\) for \(30\;\mathrm{s},\) followed by annealing at \(70\,^{\circ}\!C\) for \(3\;\mathrm{min}\) in the nitrogen glovebox. The films were then removed from the glovebox and annealed at \(100\,^{\circ}\!C\) for \(15\;\mathrm{min}\) in ambient air \(25\,^{\circ}\!C\) and \(25\%\) RH). A solution of PTAA \((40\;\mathrm{mg})\) in \(1\;\mathrm{mL}\) CB was spin-coated on the perovskite films at \(2{,}000\ \mathrm{rpm}\) for \(30~\mathrm{s}\) . Finally, an \(80\;\mathrm{nm}\;\mathrm{Ag}\) top electrode layer was added to the film by thermal evaporation under a high vacuum to obtain the \(\mathrm{ITO/SnO_{2}/P S K/P T A A/A g}\) ; PSC devices. The area of the PSCs is \(0.1\;\mathrm{cm}^{2}\) .
For hole-only devices: PEDOT:PSS films were prepared on ITO glass by spin-coating PEDOT:PSS solution at \(4{,}000\ \mathrm{rpm}\) for \(30~\mathrm{s},\) followed by annealing at \(150\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(20\;\mathrm{min}\) in air. Then, PSK films, PTAA films and Ag electrode were successively prepared in
accordance with the PSC devices fabrication method to obtain the ITO/PEDOT:PSS/PSK/PTAA/Ag devices.
## 2.3 Characterization
The PSK films, HTL films, and PSC devices were tested in air without encapsulation. The absorption spectra of the devices were obtained using a UV1700 spectrometer (SHIMADZU). Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) was conducted on a JSM7500F field-emission scanning electron microscope (JEOL) to observe the morphology of the PSK and HTL films. The surface roughness of the PSK films and HTL films were measured via atomic force microscopy (AFM) using an ICON-PT (Bruker). X-ray diffraction (XRD) was conducted using a model D/max 2400 X-ray diffraction (Bruker). Photoluminescence (PL) spectra were obtained using an RF-5301PC fluorescence spectrophotometer (SHIMADZU). Ultraviolet photoemission spectroscopy (UPS) was conducted using a 250Xi photoelectron spectrograph (Thermo Scientific Escalab). Water contact angles were obtained using a JC2000D contact angle measuring instrument (POWEREACH). The \(J{-}V\) curves of the PSCs were measured using a model 2400 digital source meter (Keithley) under AM 1.5 G simulated solar light \((100\,\mathrm{\mW{\cdot}c m^{-1}})\) (SS150 Solar Simulator, Zolix) at ambient conditions and room temperature (about \(25\,^{\circ}\!C)\) without a controlled atmosphere. The incident light intensity was calibrated with a standard Si solar cell. For the stability tests, devices were kept in ambient conditions \((30\%{-}60\%\) RH and room temperature of about \(25\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\) under dark conditions for \(1{,}000\,\mathrm{h}\) or under high-humidity conditions \((>\ 80\%\) RH). Mott–Schottky curves and electrical impedance spectra (EIP) were obtained with a Model 760E Series Electrochemical Workstation (CH Instruments Ins).
## 3 Results and discussion
In order to show the effect of 35FP doping on the morphology of PTAA, \(5.0\%\) , \(7.5\%\) and \(10.0\%\) (mass ratio with respect to
PTAA) concentrations of 35FP were introduced into PTAA. A PSK film is shown in the top-view SEM images in Figs. S3(a) and S3(b) in the ESM, demonstrating a pinhole-free and compact structure with an average grain size of \(1.10~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}\). Top-view SEM images of PSK/undoped PTAA and PSK/35FP-doped PTAA are shown in Figs. S3(c) and S3(d) in the ESM. When the PSK film is coated with PTAA, the surface of the film becomes flat. This indicates that the PTAA film completely covers the PSK film. Compared with the undoped PTAA film, the surface of the HTL coated by 35FP-doped PTAA shown in Fig. S3(d) in the ESM does not show any significant differences, demonstrating that the doping of 35FP in PTAA has no effect on the flatness of the film. In order to further observe variations in surface morphology between the undoped and doped PTAA films, two-dimensional AFM analysis was performed, shown in Fig. S4 in the ESM. The root mean square (RSM) and average surface roughness of the HTL films are tabulated in Table S1 in the ESM. The surface roughness values of the PSK/undoped PTAA and PSK/35FP-doped PTAA films are about half of that of the PSK film (PSK/PTAA: \(14.0\;\mathrm{nm}\), PSK: \(24.9\;\mathrm{nm}\)). This illustrates that the PSK film is completely covered by the PTAA HTL films, consistent with the SEM images. When PTAA is doped with 35FP, the surface roughness of the HTL decreases (PSK/5.0% 35FP-doped PTAA: \(11.2\ \mathrm{nm}\), PSK/7.5% 35FP-doped PTAA: \(9.9\ \mathrm{nm}\)). This is due to the combination of PTAA and a trace amount of 35FP serves as a hole transport layer and makes the HTL film smoother and more compact. With a further increase in the 35FP dopant concentration to \(10\%\), crystallization of excess 35FP increases the HTL surface roughness (PSK/10.0% 35FP-doped PTAA: \(12.3\ \mathrm{nm}\)).
In order to further investigate the effect of 35FP, ultraviolet photoemission spectroscopy (UPS) spectra of undoped PTAA and 35FP-doped PTAA were measured, as shown in Figs. 1(e) and 1(f). The onset \((E_{\mathrm{onset}})\) and cutoff \((E_{\mathrm{cutoff}})\) energy regions in the UPS spectra are also reported. The highest occupied molecular orbital (HOMO) position \((E_{\mathrm{HOMO}})\) is determined by using Eq. (1)
\[
E_{\mathrm{HOMO}}=h\nu-(E_{\mathrm{cutoff}}-E_{\mathrm{cutoff}})
\]
where \(h\nu\) is the He I photon energy \((21.22\ \mathrm{eV})\) [37]. The HOMO levels of undoped PTAA and \(7.5\%\) 35FP-doped PTAA are \(-4.95\) and \(-5.35\ \mathrm{eV}\), respectively, demonstrating a reduction in HOMO level with the addition of 35FP to PTAA. This indicates that the 35FP-doped PTAA film has a more suitable energy level alignment than the undoped PTAA film for collecting photogenerated holes in PSK films. Therefore, the 35FP-doped PTAA HTL has a higher open circuit voltage \((V_{\mathrm{OC}})\) [38].
An \(\mathrm{ITO/SnO_{2}/PSK/PTAA/Ag}\) device with a conventional n-i-p structure and planar heterojunction configuration was fabricated and measured to verify the effectiveness of 35FP as a PTAA dopant. The complete PSC device structure is illustrated in Fig. 2(a). Figure S5 in the ESM shows the \(J{-}V\) curves of solar cells fabricated with an undoped PTAA HTL and 35FP-doped PTAA HTLs with different concentrations of 35FP dopant. The photovoltaic parameters are summarized in Table S2 in the ESM. The PSC device with an undoped PTAA HTL exhibits a fairly low PCE of \(14.11\%\) with a short circuit current density \((J_{\mathrm{SC}})\) of \(21.54\ \mathrm{mA/cm}^{2}\), a \(V_{\mathrm{OC}}\) of \(1.02\ \mathrm{V}\), and a fill factor (FF) of \(64.23\%\). As can be seen, the PSC device performance is significantly improved by doping 35FP in PTAA. Moreover, the experimental data show that PCE increases with increasing 35FP dopant concentration up to \(7.5\%\). With an increase in 35FP dopant concentration from \(5\%\) to \(7.5\%\), \(J_{\mathrm{SC}}\) increases from \(23.69\ \mathrm{mA/cm}^{2}\) to \(24.35\ \mathrm{mA/cm}^{2}\), \(V_{\mathrm{OC}}\) increases from 1.10 to \(1.12\ \mathrm{V}\), FF increases from \(73.85\%\) to \(75.02\%\), and the PCE increases from \(19.24\%\) to \(20.45\%\). However, excess 35FP dopant concentration \((10\%)\) leads to decreased device performance. In particular, excessive 35FP increases the surface roughness of PTAA, which is not conducive to electrode deposition and leads to the significantly decrease of FF and \(V_{\mathrm{OC}}\) of the device with \(10\%\) 35FP. To provide a more intuitive observation, a statistical distribution of PSC characteristics is shown in Fig. S6 in the ESM. It is clear that the PSC devices have good repeatability, and the average PCE of the \(7.5\%\) 35FP-doped PSC \((19.50\%)\) is higher than that of the undoped device \((13.22\%)\). Overall, the PSC based on 35FP-doped PTAA demonstrates significantly enhanced performance compared to the PSC with an
undoped HTL, with a \(7.5\%\) dopant concentration demonstrating the best photovoltaic parameters.
PSCs with Li-TFSI and t-BP doped (Li-doped) PTAA were prepared to compare their performance with that of \(7.5\%\) 35FP-doped (35FP-doped) PTAA. Figure 2(b) and Table 1 show the \(J_{-}V\) curves and photovoltaic parameters of solar cells fabricated with undoped PTAA, Li-doped PTAA, and 35FP-doped PTAA. Compared with Li-doped PTAA, the \(J_{\mathrm{SC}}\), \(V_{\mathrm{OC}}\), FF, and PCE of 35FP-doped PTAA demonstrate a significant improvement. This enhanced performance indicates that 35FP is more suitable as an additive in PTAA compared with Li-TFSI and t-BP. As shown in Fig. 2(c), a PSC based on 35FP-doped PTAA demonstrates better repeatability than a PSC based on Li-doped PTAA.
In order to determine the emission properties of different HTLs, photoluminescence (PL) spectroscopy and time-resolved photoluminescence (TRPL) spectroscopy were performed. As shown in Fig. 3(a), films based on undoped PTAA and 35FP-doped PTAA exhibit emission at 801 and \(800\ \mathrm{nm}\), only demonstrating an insignificant shift in peak position. Compared with pristine PSK film, both PSK films coated with HTL show a strong quenching effect. With the addition of a dopant to PTAA, the intensity of the emission peak further decreases. Furthermore, the PL intensity of 35FP-doped PTAA is lower than that of Li-doped PTAA, meaning a faster charge extraction can be achieved. As shown in Fig. 3(b) and Table S3 in the ESM, the average PL delay time decreases from 31.32 ns for undoped PTAA to 18.29 ns for 35FP-doped PTAA. The PSC based on 35FP-doped PTAA demonstrates the shortest PL lifetime among the three tested HTLs, meaning that hole extraction from the PSK film to 35FP-doped PTAA is more efficient. Faster hole extraction and transport efficiency can suppress interface charge recombination, thereby improving PSC devices performance. Thus, From PL and TRPL results, it can be concluded that 35FP is an excellent dopant to improve the carrier transport capacity of PSCs [39, 40].
To further understand why 35FP-doped PSCs demonstrate
To further understand why 35FP-doped PSCs demonstrate improved performance, capacitance-voltage measurements were taken to study the built-in potential \((V_{\mathrm{bi}})\) of the PSC devices. As shown in Fig. 3(c), the Mott–Schottky curves \((1/C^{2}-V)\) show that the \(V_{\mathrm{bi}}\) of undoped, Li-doped, and 35FP-doped PSCs are 0.55, 0.71, and \(0.76\ \mathrm{V}\), respectively. A higher \(V_{\mathrm{bi}}\) indicates a stronger PSC electric field, which leads to faster charge carrier extraction [41, 42]. The Nyquist plots of PSC devices with different HTLs under dark conditions and the equivalent circuit model are shown in Fig. 3(d) and Fig. S7 in the ESM, gaining further insight into the effect of dopant 35FP on the performance of devices. It is well all known that large recombination resistance \((R_{\mathrm{rec}})\) means less charge recombination [41]. From Table S4 in the ESM, the \(R_{\mathrm{rec}}\) of PSCs based on 35FP-doped is significantly larger than that based on undoped and Li-doped, indicating 35FP-doped PSCs have a lower carrier recombination rates and leading to a higher FF and \(V_{\mathrm{OC}}\).
The trap density of the prepared films was measured with a space-charge-limited current (SCLC) technique. Hole-only devices with an ITO/PEDOT:PSS/PSK/PTAA/Ag structure were fabricated. The \(J_{-}V\) characteristics of these hole-only devices are shown in Figs. 4(a)–4(c). In the low bias range, the current increases linearly with increasing voltage, indicating an Ohmic-type response. As the voltage further increases, the current rapidly increases in a non-linear manner, indicating a trap-filled limit. The voltage of the point in between ohmic contact and trap-filled behavior is the trap-filled limit voltage \((V_{\mathrm{TFL}})\). The trap density can be calculated by Eq. (2)
\[
n_{\mathrm{trap}}=\frac{2\varepsilon\varepsilon_0V_{\mathrm{THL}}}{eL^2}
\]
where \(\varepsilon\) is the relative dielectric constant of the PSK film (62.23), \(\varepsilon_{0}\) is the vacuum permittivity, \(e\) is the elementary charge, and \(L\) is thickness of the PSK film (860 nm as shown in Fig. S8 in the ESM) [43–45]. According to Eq. (2), the trap density is proportional to \(V_{\mathrm{THL}}\). Figures 4(a)–4(c) show that the \(V_{\mathrm{THL}}\) values of undoped PTAA, Li-doped PTAA, and 35FP-doped PTAA are 0.73, 0.57,
and \(0.47\,\mathrm{~V~}\), respectively. This indicates that the trap density of 35FP-doped PTAA \((4.37\times10^{15}\,\mathrm{cm}^{-3})\) as an HTL is lower than that of undoped PTAA \((6.79\times10^{15}\,\mathrm{cm}^{-3})\) and Li-doped PTAA \((5.30\times10^{15}\,\mathrm{cm}^{-3})\). The 15 strongly electronegative fluorine atoms in 35FP form a stronger center of positive charge at the central phosphorus atom, so it can be used as a strong Lewis acid to passivate PSK defects [46]. This is helpful for reducing nonradiative recombination and improving \(V_{\mathrm{OC}}\) [41].
To further understand the performance enhancement of PSCs based on 35FP-doped PTAA, the \(I{-}V\) characteristics of an ITO/PTAA/Ag structure with undoped, Li-doped, and 35FP-doped PTAA as the HTL were measured. As shown in Fig. 4(d), 35FP-doped PTAA has a higher slope than the other two devices. According to the conductivity calculation formula, the conductivity of the 35FP-doped PTAA film \((4.28\,\times\,10^{-5}\,\,\mathrm{S/cm})\) is higher than that of undoped PTAA \((6.05\,\times\,10^{-6}\,\,\mathrm{S/cm})\) and Li-doped PTAA \((2.81\,\times\,10^{-5}\,\,\mathrm{S/cm})\) films. This indicates that the migration efficiency of photogenerated carriers from the PSK film to 35FP-doped PTAA is higher than that to undoped PTAA or Li-doped PTAA. The faster charge extraction from the PSK film to the 35FP-doped HTL results in a stronger PL quenching effect, consistent with the PL spectra in Fig. 3(a) [47].
Dark \(J{-}V\) curves of the PSC devices with undoped PTAA, Li
Dark \(J{-}V\) curves of the PSC devices with undoped PTAA, Li-doped PTAA, and 35FP-doped PTAA were measured under dark conditions. The effect of the additives on the interfacial recombination of PSCs was studied with the Schockley diode equation. As shown in Fig. 4(e), the PSC device with 35FP-doped PTAA shows a lower dark current density compared to the PSCs with undoped PTAA and Li-doped PTAA. This implies that the 35FP dopant in PTAA can significantly reduce leakage current and decrease the loss of interfacial charge recombination [41, 48]. To further understand the influence of 35FP-doped PTAA on the PSCs, the photocurrent density \((J_{\mathrm{ph}})\) vs. effective voltage \((V_{\mathrm{eff}})\) of the devices based on undoped, Li-doped, and 35FP-doped PTAA were plotted, shown in Fig. 4(f). \(J_{\mathrm{ph}}\) is defined by Eq. (3)
\[
J_{\mathrm{ph}}=J_{\mathrm{L}}-J_{\mathrm{D}}
\]
where \(J_{\mathrm{L}}\) and \(J_{\mathrm{D}}\) are the current density under illuminated and dark conditions, respectively. \(V_{\mathrm{eff}}\) is defined by Eq. (4)
\[
V_{\mathrm{eff}}=V_{0}-V_{\mathrm{L}}
\]
where \(V_{0}\) and \(V_{\mathrm{a}}\) are the voltage at \(J_{\mathrm{L}}=J_{\mathrm{D}}\) and the applied voltage, respectively. Figure 5(f) shows that compared with the other two devices, the \(J_{\mathrm{ph}}\) of the 35FP-doped PSC increases to reach the saturation photocurrent \((J_{\mathrm{sat}})\) at a lower \(V_{\mathrm{eff}}\). This result suggests that the dependence of the electric field of the 35FP-doped PSC on the carrier transport is low, leading to high \(J_{\mathrm{SC}}\) and FF. Furthermore, the ratio of \(J_{\mathrm{ph}}\) to \(J_{\mathrm{sat}}\) determines the overall efficiencies of exciton and charge collection [49, 50]. It is clear that the \(J_{\mathrm{ph}}/J_{\mathrm{sat}}\) ratio of the PSC based on 35FP-doped PTAA is higher than that of PSCs based on undoped and Li-doped PTAA when \(V_{\mathrm{eff}}\) is constant. These results clarify that the addition of 35FP is beneficial for improving charge extraction and suppressing carrier recombination.
The water contact angles of the undoped and doped PTAA films were investigated to understand the effect of the dopants on the wettability behavior of PTAA, as shown in Fig. 5. The undoped and 35FP-doped HTL films have water contact angles of \(87.9^{\circ}\) and \(93.4^{\circ}\), respectively. The hydrophobicity is one of the criteria of device stability. The long-term stability of the 35FP-doped HTL device compared with the undoped HTL device may be attributed to the hydrophobicity of 35FP. In addition, The Li-doped PTAA film has a water contact angle of \(84.7^{\circ}\), lower than that of undoped PTAA. This demonstrates its reduced hydrophobicity, which may be caused by the hygroscopicity of Li-TFSI. Therefore, when PSCs with Li-doped PTAA is used in long-term applications, their HTL and PSK films rapidly degrade due to the Li salt dopant [39, 51].
In order to more intuitively observe the water barrier effect of the PTAA HTL doped with 35FP, the film decomposition rate of PSK/undoped PTAA, PSK/Li-doped PTAA and PSK/35FP-doped PTAA was recorded when water droplets were placed on the HTL film surface. The decomposition images for these films under water droplets are shown in Figs. S9–S11 in the ESM. Initially, the decomposition rate of the 35FP-doped PTAA film was about \(25\%\) slower than that of the undoped PTAA film. After the HTL films degraded, the degradation rates of the two PSK films were approximately the same. However, after water drops on the sample surface, the Li-doped PTAA film is rapidly destroyed due to the hygroscopicity of Li-TFSI. Therefore, 35FP can well prevent water damage to PSK, which more conducive to the long-term use of PSCs.
To gain insight into the effects of 35FP doping on the long-term stability of the PSC devices, as-synthesized films and films stored in air for \(1{,}000\,\mathrm{~h~}\) were investigated by XRD measurements. Figures 6(a)–6(c) show that the fresh films have diffraction peaks at \(13.7^{\circ}\), \(19.7^{\circ}\), \(24.1^{\circ}\), \(27.9^{\circ}\), \(31.7^{\circ}\), \(40.1^{\circ}\), and \(42.6^{\circ}\), corresponding to the (001), (010), (111), (002), (012), (022), and (003) planes of \(\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3}\), respectively [52, 53]. Furthermore, a diffraction peak attributed to \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) appears at \(12.4^{\circ}\). This diffraction peak is due to the \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) in the first spin-coating step not completely converting to \(\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3}\) PSK film and the organic amine solution in the second spin-coating step. Thelakat and co-workers have confirmed that a small amount of excess \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) can improve the initial performance of PSCs [54]. XRD spectra in the low-angle region show a diffraction peak of \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) and the (001) peak of \(\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3}\), shown in Figs. S12(a)–S12(c) in the ESM. The crystallinity of the measured samples are related to the peak intensity and full width at half maximum (FWHM) of XRD diffraction peaks. The fresh films show similar intensities and FWHM values. However, after 1,000 h of exposure to air, the PSK film coated with 35FP-doped PTAA has a smaller FWHM and stronger intensity than the PSK film coated with undoped PTAA. According to the scherrer formula, the mean grain size of the undoped PSCs changed from 52.94 to
\(39.61\ \mathrm{nm}\) after exposure to air for \(1{,}000\,\mathrm{h}\), while that of the 35FP-doped PSCs changed from 49.47 to \(44.61\ \mathrm{nm}\). The grain size change rates of undoped and 35FP-doped devices are \(25.18\%\) and \(9.41\%\), respectively. In addition, the introduction of 35FP lead to slow down of the intensity increase trend of the \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) diffraction peak after \(1{,}000\ \mathrm{h}\) of exposure to air. These results indicate that doping PTAA with the hydrophobic fluorine ions of 35FP allows the penetration of water molecules to be more effectively blocked in the hole transport layer. However, due to the hygroscopicity of Li-TFSI, the Li-doped PTAA films are destroyed. It causes the surface of the perovskite to contact water and the perovskite to hydrolyze. Therefore, it can be seen from Fig. 6(b), the PSK film in the Li-doped sample is almost completely decomposed into \(\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) after \(1{,}000\ \mathrm{h}\) of exposure to air.
Finally, the long-term stability of PSCs based on undoped PTAA, Li-doped PTAA, and 35FP-doped PTAA without encapsulation were tested under air exposure \((30\%-60\%\) RH and a room temperature of about \(25\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\) for \(1{,}000\ \mathrm{h}\) and in a high-humidity environment \((>80\%\) RH). As shown in Fig. 6(c) and Fig. S13 in the ESM, the 35FP-based PSC device retained about \(80\%\) of its initial efficiency after \(1{,}000\ \mathrm{h}\) under air exposure. In contrast, the PCE of the undoped PSC device sharply dropped, only retaining about \(20\%\) of its original efficiency after \(1{,}000\ \mathrm{h}\). Additionally, due to the deliquescent and hygroscopic properties of Li-TFSI and the volatility of t-BP, the PCE of the Li-doped PSC device declined to less than \(20\%\) of its initial value after \(640\ \mathrm{h}\) of air exposure. The results of PSCs tested in the high-humidity environment are shown in Fig. 6(d). After \(360\ \mathrm{h}\) of exposure to high humidity, the 35FP-doped PSC maintained about \(60\%\) of its initial PCE while the PCE of the undoped PSC declined to less than \(20\%\) of its initial value. In good agreement with the air exposure experiment, the efficiency of the Li-doped PSC device declined to less than \(20\%\) of its initial efficiency after \(290\ \mathrm{h}\) of high-humidity storage. The excellent stability of the 35FP-based PSC device can be attributed to the hydrophobicity of the 35FP dopant and uniformity of the resulting HTL layer, resulting in tightly
protected HTL and perovskite layers that are resistant to air degradation. In addition, as a strong Lewis acid, 35FP can effectively passivate the surface defects of PSK, and also improve the stability of PSCs. In contrast, the poor stability of the undoped and Li-doped PSCs leads to degradation of the perovskite and HTL.
## 4 Conclusions
In summary, we investigated 35FP as an organic hole transport material dopant, demonstrating that 35FP-doped PTAA increases charge extraction, reduces trap density, and improves the performance of corresponding PSC devices. The PSC with a \(7.5\%\) 35FP-doped PTAA demonstrated excellent performance (PCE \(=20.45\%\) ) compared to a control device with undoped PTAA (PCE \(=14.11\%\) ). In addition, because 35FP contains a large number of hydrophobic fluorine ions, the long-term stability of these PSC devices was significantly enhanced. For the PSC device based on 35FP-doped PTAA, only decline about \(20\%\) of its original efficiency after 1,000 h under ambient conditions without encapsulation. It was much better that that of PSC devices based on undoped or Li-doped PTAA. The excellent environmental stability of 35FP-based PSCs can be attributed to their high hydrophobicity, uniform HTL layer, lack of aggregation, and good crystallization.
